    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DNS protocol specification summary</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Protocol attribute</th><th>Technical specification</th><th>Standard / RFC</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Core function</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Hostname-to-IP resolution, mail routing, cryptographic domain verification</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 1034 / RFC 1035</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Default transport</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">UDP port 53 for queries up to 512 bytes; up to 4096 bytes with EDNS0</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 1035 / RFC 6891</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Fallback transport</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TCP port 53 for truncated responses and zone transfers</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 7766 / RFC 5936</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Encrypted transports</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DoH port 443, DoT port 853, DoQ over QUIC</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 8484 / RFC 7858 / RFC 9250</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Name length limits</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">63 characters per label, 253 characters total</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 1035</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Response codes</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">NOERROR (0), FORMERR (1), SERVFAIL (2), NXDOMAIN (3), REFUSED (5)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 6895</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Core DNS record types and what each controls</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Code</th><th>What it maps</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">A</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">1</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Hostname to IPv4 address</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Websites</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">AAAA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">28</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Hostname to IPv6 address</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Websites on IPv6</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">CNAME</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">5</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Alias to another hostname</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Subdomain shortcuts</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">MX</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">15</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Domain to mail server with priority</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Email routing</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">TXT</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">16</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Free-form text</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SPF, DKIM, DMARC, site verification</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">NS</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">2</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Domain to authoritative nameservers</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Delegation</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">SOA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">6</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Zone authority and timing values</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Zone management</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">PTR</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">12</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">IP address back to hostname</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Reverse DNS</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">CAA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">257</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Allowed certificate authorities</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">TLS issuance control</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DS / DNSKEY</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">43 / 48</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Delegation and zone signing keys</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DNSSEC</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DNS history timeline</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Milestone</th><th>Engineering change</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">1971-1982</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">HOSTS.TXT at SRI NIC</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">One flat file mapped every host on the ARPANET</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">1983</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 882 / RFC 883</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Paul Mockapetris designs the distributed Domain Name System</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">1987</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 1034 / RFC 1035</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Modern DNS: record types, caching, hierarchy standardized</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">1998</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 2308 + ICANN</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Negative caching defined; internet governance formalized</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">2005</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 4033-4035</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DNSSEC adds cryptographic signing to the hierarchy</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">2014-2015</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 7208 / RFC 7489</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SPF and DMARC standardize DNS-based email authentication</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">2018</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 8484</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DNS-over-HTTPS encrypts queries inside browser-native traffic</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">2023</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">RFC 9460</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SVCB and HTTPS records enable service binding</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
    <Diagram name="resolution" caption="The DNS resolution path from browser to website" />

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-problem-dns-solves">The problem DNS solves</h2>
      <p>
        Every device on the internet is reachable by an IP address. IPv4 addresses look like
        <code>192.0.2.1</code>; IPv6 addresses look like <code>2606:4700::1111</code>. Humans are
        terrible at remembering numbers and great at remembering names, so in 1983, Paul Mockapetris
        designed a global, distributed database that maps names to numbers. Forty-plus years later,
        the same basic design runs the entire internet.
      </p>

      <h2 id="a-hierarchy-not-one-computer">A hierarchy, not one computer</h2>
      <p>DNS is a tree with four levels involved in every lookup:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Root servers</strong>, 13 logical names (hundreds of machines worldwide) that know where TLD servers live.</li>
        <li><strong>TLD servers</strong>, responsible for each top-level domain (<code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code>, <code>.dev</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Authoritative nameservers</strong>, your domain's actual DNS host, holding the zone with all its records.</li>
        <li><strong>Recursive resolvers</strong>, the "librarians" (like Cloudflare 1.1.1.1 or Google 8.8.8.8) that walk the tree on your behalf and cache the answers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="more-than-websites">More than websites</h2>
      <p>
        DNS doesn't just point domains at servers. Records decide <strong>where your email goes</strong>
        (MX), <strong>who may send as your domain</strong> (SPF, DKIM, DMARC), <strong>which certificate
        authorities may issue your TLS certificates</strong> (CAA), and whether your zone's answers are
        cryptographically signed (DNSSEC). When any of these break, the symptom is rarely "DNS error",
        it's a site that won't load, or mail that lands in spam.
      </p>

      <h2 id="check-it-yourself-right-now">Check it yourself, right now</h2>
      <p>
        The fastest way to understand DNS is to watch it work. Run any domain through our
        <a href="/dns-record-lookup/">DNS record lookup</a> to see exactly what the system returns,
        or the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> to see how different
        resolvers answer for the same name.
      </p>

      <h2 id="key-facts">Key facts</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Created in 1983 (RFC 882/883), standardized as RFC 1034/1035 in 1987.</li>
        <li>Runs on UDP/TCP port 53 classically; encrypted as DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) or DNS-over-TLS (DoT) today.</li>
        <li>Caching with TTLs is why changes "propagate" rather than appearing instantly.</li>
      </ul>

